fact WorkItem(id: "WI-391", description: some(value: "Decide the interpretation of 'fact Spec[...]' vs 'provides Spec[...]' (re-filed; was a local WI-389 that collided with the remote's WI-389/HandlerAction on sync). The two emit DIFFERENT SortProvidesInfo specs for CONCRETE ground bindings: 'fact Stream[T=Int]' lowers T to Ref(Int) via parse convert_type_value, but 'provides Stream[T=Int]' lowers T to name_to_sort_term(Int) (a nullary Fn) via sort_inst_to_value (empirically TermId 2583 vs 12; WI-366 B1 probe). WI-366 B1 already fixed the LOSS half (a written effect-row {} no longer dropped on the fact head); THIS is the distinct NON-LOSS half. OPEN DESIGN QUESTION FIRST (user) -- do NOT presuppose byte-identity is the goal; the difference MAY be correct: step 0 is to DECIDE how to interpret a fact relative to a provides. WHY IT MAY BE LEGITIMATE (user): on the FACT (term) path we do NOT KNOW that 'Int' is a TYPE -- convert_type_value lowers the binding as an OPAQUE NAME (Ref), since a 'fact Spec[T=Int]'s bracket bindings are logical terms with no type-checking context; the PROVIDES (type-aware) path knows it's a type, so lowers it as a sort/type term (name_to_sort_term -> nullary Fn). So the Ref-vs-Fn divergence reflects a type-KNOWLEDGE asymmetry. DECIDE: do a fact's bracket bindings carry type info (-> unify the two paths, route fact-head emission through sort_inst_to_value, byte-identical SortProvidesInfo) OR are they deliberately untyped logical terms (-> the difference is CORRECT; document the intended semantics of each and close)? Acceptance is CONDITIONAL on that decision, not 'make byte-identical' unconditionally. Interacts with kernel-language.md sec.1418 (fact->SortProvidesInfo convention) and the wi210/wi357 dispatch canaries (which already rely on Ref=wildcard vs Fn=concrete -- evidence the difference may be intentional). Relates: WI-366 B1 (predecessor), WI-387 (aligned the type-PARAM half T=T -> Ref on both)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-13T10:56:05Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-391", author: "user", content: "EXTRACTABILITY CRITERION ADDED (2026-06-11, user; design path-dependent-types.md sec.5.3 'Extractability criterion'): no type is ever represented as an opaque value -- every type value's backing term must classify into exactly one STRUCTURAL TypeExtractor variant; TypeExtractor.Error is reserved for malformed USER input, never a system-minted shape. This is DECISION PRESSURE for this ticket: the nullary Fn{S} that name_to_sort_term stores for provides/requires binding values extracts as ERROR -- whatever the fact-side untyped-term reading decides, a binding value USED IN A TYPE POSITION must lower to the extractable type shape (Ref(S) / Fn{S, named}), not the opaque nullary functor. WI-428's normalize_ground_leaf is the criterion's LOCAL fix at one consumer (delta-grounding); this ticket's decision is the global one. ACCEPTANCE ADDITION: after the decision, extract() over every stored type-position value in the loaded stdlib+examples yields a non-Error variant.", at: "2026-06-11T05:49:28Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-391", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED 2026-06-13 (commits 0bc440f + review cleanup 4bb93a2; suite 466 wi_tests green). DECISION (user 'normalize early'): the fact-vs-provides binding-shape divergence is an IMPLEMENTATION ARTIFACT, not a semantics -- dispatch is KIND-aware (dispatch_values_match -> is_sort_param_symbol reads the symbol's kind: Ref(Int)=concrete, Ref(T)=wildcard, tolerating BOTH shapes), and the kernel treats fact/provides as duals. FIX (normalize early at the PRODUCER): new sort_binding_to_value lowers a concrete bare-sort binding VALUE to the canonical Ref(S) -- the one extractable bare-sort shape (type_head: a nullary Fn{S} is malformed -> TypeExtractor::Error), byte-identical to the fact path's parse Ref. The spec-IDENTITY slot (bare 'provides Spec') keeps the Fn{Spec} functor unwrap_spec_view needs; only the binding-VALUE position normalizes. Subsumes WI-387 (aligned only the T=T param half). PAYOFF: the 4 late normalize_ground_leaf consumer-patches are now DEAD BY CONSTRUCTION (no producer emits a nullary-Fn binding value) -- probe-proven (0 actual transforms across the whole suite incl. stdlib + SortAlias targets), RETIRED + function removed. normalize_spec_binding_type KEPT (live structured-binding gate at resolve_rigid_projection + bare_provider_binding_precise). /code-review (max effort, 3 finders): NO correctness bug, NO consumer breakage -- both finders independently confirmed every binding reader is shape-agnostic for concrete sorts (sort_sym_of_term/is_type_param_value/values_structurally_equal treat Ref==Fn; specificity scoring routes both through the same arm); fixed 2 stale doc refs. Tests: wi391_binding_extractability_test (fixture: concrete binding extracts SortRef + fact/provides BYTE-IDENTICAL; stdlib-wide sweep: every binding non-Error). DEFERRED -> WI-449 (filed, typing tag, deps WI-391, user-decided): FACT-path positional/nested parameterized binding canonicalization -- (1) fact Effect[T = Modify[?]] -> positional Fn{Modify, pos:[?]} still Error (sweep bounds+tolerates structurally); (2) convert_instantiation_term FLATTENS nested Inner[Int64] -> Ref(Inner) lossily (pre-existing, diverges from provides). WI-391 unified the BARE-SORT half; WI-449 = the parameterized half.", at: "2026-06-13T10:56:07Z")
